"""Slide text and handout explanations for the five clustering lectures."""

LECTURES = [
    ("K-Means", "Find groups in unlabeled observations, fit centroids and decide how many groups are useful.",
     ["7.1 Clustering", "7.2 K-Means", "7.2.1 Initialization", "7.2.2 Number of clusters"],
     r"""
@7.1 · Clustering
unlabeled | Could we group apples and pears without labels? | Imagine a basket of mixed fruit. Shape, color, and taste give us clues about which pieces belong together, even before anyone names the groups. | In classification, a target label tells us the category to learn. In clustering, shape, color, and taste are all features. The task is to discover groups whose members resemble one another and differ from those in other groups.
features | From measurements to feature vectors | Each observation is a row of measurements. The same $m$ features describe every row. | $X_j$ is the $j$-th feature variable. $x_{ij}$ is the value of feature $j$ for observation $i$. A distance compares two rows in this feature space. | X=(X_1,\ldots,X_m),\qquad x_i=(x_{i1},\ldots,x_{im})\in\mathbb R^m
data-lab | How many groups do you see? | Inspect this unlabeled cloud. Which points seem close to one another, and where are the gaps? | The coordinate axes are two features. Pan or zoom, then return to the full plot. Three compact regions are visible at this scale. | @source:kmeans_data
meaning | What makes a collection of points a cluster? | Points within a group should be similar. Points in different groups should be relatively dissimilar. | A grouping depends on which features and distance are useful for the task. With no supplied targets, accuracy against known labels is unavailable during fitting. Domain knowledge and geometric checks help interpret the result. | C_1,\ldots,C_K
@7.2 · K-Means
centroids | Represent each group by its centroid | Assume there are $K$ groups. Represent group $k$ by an average observation, its centroid $\mu_k$. | A centroid has the same $m$ coordinates as a data row. It can lie between observations. K-means learns the centroids together with the group assignments. | \mu_k\in\mathbb R^m,\qquad k=1,\ldots,K
one-hot | Record membership with exactly one 1 | If observation $i$ belongs to cluster $C_k$, put a 1 in position $k$ of its one-hot row $r_i=(r_{i1},\ldots,r_{iK})$. | $r_{ik}$ is a binary membership flag. Three groups and membership in group 2 give $r_i=(0,1,0)$. All $n$ rows form the assignment array $\boldsymbol r$. | r_{ik}=\begin{cases}1 & \text{if }x_i\in C_k\\0 & \text{otherwise}\end{cases},\qquad\sum_{k=1}^K r_{ik}=1
objective | What are the two K-means steps trying to minimize? | From initial centroids, assign points to the nearest centroid, then recalculate each centroid as its group mean. Repeat to reduce the distortion $J$. | The double sum adds the squared distance of each of the $n$ observations to its assigned centroid. The constraint keeps each row one-hot. The hats mark the minimizing centroids and assignments. | \begin{aligned}(\hat{\boldsymbol\mu},\hat{\boldsymbol r})&=\operatorname*{argmin}_{\boldsymbol\mu\in\mathbb R^{K\times m},\ \boldsymbol r\in\{0,1\}^{n\times K}} J(\boldsymbol\mu,\boldsymbol r)\\&=\operatorname*{argmin}_{\boldsymbol\mu\in\mathbb R^{K\times m},\ \boldsymbol r\in\{0,1\}^{n\times K}}\sum_{i=1}^n\sum_{k=1}^K r_{ik}\|x_i-\mu_k\|^2\\&\text{subject to }\sum_{k=1}^K r_{ik}=1\quad(i=1,\ldots,n)\end{aligned}
distortion-example | Which terms enter the distortion? | Fix two centroids at 1 and 5. Inspect one point at a time to see its distances, its assignment and its contribution to $J$. | The 0 entries in its membership row turn off the distances to the other centroids. A tie can be broken by a fixed rule. | %assignment
assign | Step 1 fixes the centroids | With the centers held still, minimize the distortion by choosing only the membership flags. | The one-hot constraint applies to each row, independently of the other observations. This is a discrete comparison of squared distances. | \begin{aligned}\hat{\boldsymbol r}&=\operatorname*{argmin}_{\boldsymbol r\in\{0,1\}^{n\times K}}J(\boldsymbol\mu,\boldsymbol r)\\&=\operatorname*{argmin}_{\boldsymbol r\in\{0,1\}^{n\times K}}\sum_{i=1}^n\sum_{k=1}^K r_{ik}\|x_i-\mu_k\|^2\\&\text{subject to }\sum_{k=1}^K r_{ik}=1\quad(i=1,\ldots,n)\end{aligned}
nearest-rule | Pick the smallest distance in each row | For a fixed observation, turning on the flag of its closest centroid gives the smallest contribution to $J$. | Read the argmin as “the index $\ell$ with the smallest distance”. If two distances are exactly equal, pick one according to a consistent tie rule. | r_{ik}=\begin{cases}1&\text{if } k=\operatorname*{argmin}_{\ell\in\{1,\ldots,K\}}\|x_i-\mu_\ell\|^2\\0&\text{otherwise}\end{cases}
update | Step 2 fixes the assignments | Once the rows are assigned, choose a new location for each centroid. | Every term containing $\mu_k$ is a squared distance with a fixed membership flag. Changing $\mu_k$ only affects the points whose flag for group $k$ is 1. | \begin{aligned}\hat{\boldsymbol\mu}&=\operatorname*{argmin}_{\boldsymbol\mu\in\mathbb R^{K\times m}}J(\boldsymbol\mu,\boldsymbol r)\\&=\operatorname*{argmin}_{\boldsymbol\mu\in\mathbb R^{K\times m}}\sum_{i=1}^n\sum_{k=1}^K r_{ik}\|x_i-\mu_k\|^2\end{aligned}
mean-derivative | Why does the mean minimize distortion? | Differentiate with respect to $\mu_k$. At the minimum, set the gradient to zero and collect the terms containing $\mu_k$. | The denominator is the number of points in $C_k$. When the cluster is occupied, the objective for its centroid is a convex quadratic with this unique minimum. An empty cluster needs a separate handling rule. | \begin{gathered}\frac{\partial J}{\partial\mu_k}=2\sum_{i=1}^n r_{ik}(\mu_k-x_i)\\\frac{\partial J}{\partial\mu_k}=0\;\Longrightarrow\;\mu_k\sum_{i=1}^n r_{ik}=\sum_{i=1}^n r_{ik}x_i\;\Longrightarrow\;\mu_k=\frac{\sum_{i=1}^n r_{ik}x_i}{\sum_{i=1}^n r_{ik}}\end{gathered}
mean-example | Move a centroid to its group mean | Drag the trial center for the points 0, 2 and 4. Where does their sum of squared distances reach its minimum? | The mean is $(0+2+4)/3=2$. At that position the squared distances are 4, 0 and 4. | %mean
source-animation | Watch the centroids settle | Advance through the five frames. Track one cross and the points with its color. | Crosses mark centroids. A point can switch groups as the centers move. In this example the visible groups settle after a few rounds. | @frames:kmeans_steps
kmeans-lab | Inspect assignment and movement separately | Use Step to see which changes first. Then try a different initialization or change $K$. | Each of the two operations holds the other set of choices fixed. The final $J$ measures how close the points are to their own fitted centers. | @kmeans
@7.2.1 · Initialization
initialization | The starting centroids can change the answer | The two steps keep improving or preserving $J$, and different starts can end at different local solutions. | Run several initializations for the same $K$ and retain the fit with the lowest distortion among the runs. The geometry implied by the features and distance still matters when you interpret the result.
@7.2.2 · Number of clusters
choose-k | Why does distortion keep falling with $K$? | More centroids give the algorithm more freedom. With $K=n$, each observation can be its own centroid and the distortion can reach zero. | The best achievable distortion cannot increase with $K$. In finite optimization runs, weak initializations can obscure that trend. Zero distortion at $K=n$ does not tell us whether the grouping is useful.
@7.2.2.1 · Elbow method
elbow-source | Where does the distortion curve begin to flatten? | Fit the same data for each $K$. The elbow is where large drops in distortion give way to smaller gains. | The plotted highlight marks a candidate bend. An elbow may be weak or absent when the curve falls gradually. A useful choice should also make sense in the application. | @source:elbow
@7.2.2.2 · Silhouette method
silhouette | First measure how close a point is to its own group | Compute $a_K(x_i)$ by averaging the squared distances to the other members of $C(x_i)$. | $C(x_i)$ is the assigned cluster. $i^{\prime}$ indexes neighboring observations. Exclude the point itself from the numerator and denominator. | a_K(x_i)=\bar d(x_i,C(x_i))=\frac{1}{|C(x_i)|-1}\sum_{\substack{x_{i^{\prime}}\in C(x_i)\\i^{\prime}\ne i}}\|x_{i^{\prime}}-x_i\|^2
silhouette-other | Then find the nearest alternative group | Average the squared distances to the points in each other group. Keep the smallest of these averages, $b_K(x_i)$. | Compare whole groups through their average distance. A single close point in another cluster does not automatically make that cluster the closest alternative. | b_K(x_i)=\min_{C_k\ne C(x_i)}\bar d(x_i,C_k)=\min_{C_k\ne C(x_i)}\frac{1}{|C_k|}\sum_{x_{i^{\prime}}\in C_k}\|x_{i^{\prime}}-x_i\|^2
silhouette-reading | How do we read a silhouette value? | Toward +1 means the point is much closer to its own group. Around 0 means the two averages are similar. Below 0 means another group is closer on average. | A singleton gets 0 by convention. Use 0 also when both averages vanish. A silhouette comparison requires at least two clusters. | s(x_i)=\begin{cases}\dfrac{b_K(x_i)-a_K(x_i)}{\max(a_K(x_i),b_K(x_i))}&\text{if }|C(x_i)|>1\text{ and }\max(a_K(x_i),b_K(x_i))>0\\0&\text{otherwise}\end{cases},\quad s(x_i)\in[-1,1]
silhouette-lab | Inspect one observation at a time | Change the inspected point. Compare its own-group average, nearest alternative and silhouette. Which points sit near a boundary? | The default uses squared Euclidean dissimilarity, matching the equations. The optional Euclidean view shows how a different convention can change the numbers. | @silhouette
silhouette-coefficient | Compare the mean silhouette across candidate values of $K$ | Calculate each observation’s score, then average the scores for that fitted partition. Choose the $K$ with the largest mean among the candidates. | $\bar s_K$ averages individual scores at a given $K$. The silhouette coefficient $SC$ is the largest of those averages across the tested candidates where the score is defined. | SC=\max_K \bar s_K,\qquad \bar s_K=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n s(x_i)
selection-lab | Choose $K$ by comparing entire partitions | Switch between distortion, mean silhouette and the partition. Does the elbow agree with the largest mean silhouette? | The two criteria reward different aspects of the same partitions. Interpret a candidate alongside the plotted groups and the goal of the analysis. | @selection
"""),
    ("Gaussian Mixture Clustering", "Start from soft membership, build a Gaussian mixture and learn its parameters with Expectation–Maximization.",
     ["7.3 Gaussian Mixture Clustering", "7.3.1 Training", "7.4 Expectation-Maximization"],
     r"""
@7.3 · Gaussian Mixture Clustering
soft | What if a point could belong to either group? | K-means makes a hard assignment to one group. If clouds overlap, a soft assignment keeps the probability of each possible membership. | $Y$ is the unobserved cluster index. $X$ is the feature vector and $x$ is the observed row. For example, membership probabilities 0.6 and 0.4 retain information about ambiguity. | \mathbb P(Y=k\mid X=x)
bayes-mixture | Use Bayes’ theorem to obtain soft membership | Turn a component’s density and prior weight into a probability of membership after seeing the point. | $y$ is a possible value of $Y$. The numerator combines the component likelihood and prior. The evidence in the denominator normalizes across all components. In classification we could learn from known labels. Here the cluster index is hidden, so the components and their prior weights must be learned from the observed features. | p_{Y\mid X}(y\mid x)=\frac{p_{X\mid Y}(x\mid y)\,p_Y(y)}{p_X(x)}
gaussian | Model each cluster with a Gaussian | The mean $\mu_k$ places the component. The covariance matrix $\Sigma_k$ controls how the observations spread around that mean. | We model all $m$ features jointly. A covariance matrix is positive semidefinite. A nonsingular Gaussian density in the full feature space needs a positive definite covariance. | \begin{gathered}X\mid Y=k\sim\mathcal N(\mu_k,\Sigma_k),\qquad p_{X\mid Y}(x\mid k)=\mathcal N(x;\mu_k,\Sigma_k)\\\mu_k\in\mathbb R^m,\quad\Sigma_k\in\mathbb R^{m\times m}\end{gathered}
covariance-component | Read the entries of $\Sigma_k$ | The diagonal gives individual feature variances. Off-diagonal entries say how pairs of features move together within cluster $k$. | $j$ and $j^{\prime}$ index features. Positive covariance tilts a two-feature cloud toward an upward diagonal. Negative covariance tilts it toward a downward diagonal. | (\Sigma_k)_{jj^{\prime}}=\begin{cases}\operatorname{Var}(X_j\mid Y=k)&\text{if }j=j^{\prime}\\\operatorname{Cov}(X_j,X_{j^{\prime}}\mid Y=k)&\text{otherwise}\end{cases}
mixture-prior | Give each Gaussian a share of the mixture | Before seeing a particular point, $\pi_k$ gives the probability of its membership in component $k$. | With three equally weighted components, each prior is $1/3$. When one component is more common, its weight can be larger. The component weights are learned. | p_Y(k)=\pi_k,\qquad\pi_k\ge 0,\qquad\sum_{k=1}^K\pi_k=1
mixture | Add the weighted densities to find the evidence | The point may have come from any component. Add each possible source’s density contribution. | This is the law of total probability applied to density. The mixture is a convex combination of Gaussian densities and can have several peaks. | \begin{aligned}p_X(x)&=\sum_{y=1}^K p_{X,Y}(x,y)\\&=\sum_{y=1}^K p_{X\mid Y}(x\mid y)p_Y(y)\\&=\sum_{y=1}^K\pi_y\mathcal N(x;\mu_y,\Sigma_y)\end{aligned}
mixture-shape | Can two Gaussian components look like one peak? | Change the separation or the prior weight. Compare the two weighted contributions with their sum. | A component is a source in the model. A peak is a shape in the resulting density. Strong overlap can hide separate components in the summed curve. | %density
posterior | Divide one contribution by the sum | The resulting number says what fraction of the density at this observation came from component $k$. | The $y$ in the denominator runs over all components. A hard label is optional. Taking the argmax selects the index with the largest posterior. | \begin{gathered}p_{Y\mid X}(k\mid x)=\frac{\pi_k\mathcal N(x;\mu_k,\Sigma_k)}{\sum_{y=1}^K\pi_y\mathcal N(x;\mu_y,\Sigma_y)}\\c=\operatorname*{argmax}_{k\in\{1,\ldots,K\}}p_{Y\mid X}(k\mid x)\end{gathered}
posterior-example | Calculate one posterior by hand | Inspect an observation and change the prior. How much of the total density belongs to each component at that location? | If the weighted densities are 0.12 and 0.08, then the evidence is 0.20 and the posterior of component 1 is $0.12/0.20=0.60$. The two posteriors sum to 1. | %posterior
@7.3.1 · Training
likelihood | Fit the parameters that make the observations plausible | Given a dataset $\mathcal D=\{x_i\}_{i=1}^n$, use the joint likelihood of its observations to estimate the mixture parameters. | $\boldsymbol\pi,\boldsymbol\mu,\boldsymbol\Sigma$ collect parameters for all $K$ components. $\boldsymbol X$ denotes the observed dataset in the likelihood. The mixture likelihood integrates out the unknown label of each observation. | (\hat{\boldsymbol\pi},\hat{\boldsymbol\mu},\hat{\boldsymbol\Sigma})=\operatorname*{argmax}_{\boldsymbol\pi,\boldsymbol\mu,\boldsymbol\Sigma}p(\boldsymbol X\mid\boldsymbol\pi,\boldsymbol\mu,\boldsymbol\Sigma)
likelihood-product | From the dataset likelihood to a sum over observations | A logarithm preserves the maximizing parameters. Independent observations give a product, and the log turns that product into a sum. | The maximizing parameters are the same in all three lines. The natural log makes the calculation easier by giving each observed row one term in a sum. | \begin{aligned}(\hat{\boldsymbol\pi},\hat{\boldsymbol\mu},\hat{\boldsymbol\Sigma})&=\operatorname*{argmax}_{\boldsymbol\pi,\boldsymbol\mu,\boldsymbol\Sigma}\ln p(\boldsymbol X\mid\boldsymbol\pi,\boldsymbol\mu,\boldsymbol\Sigma)\\&=\operatorname*{argmax}_{\boldsymbol\pi,\boldsymbol\mu,\boldsymbol\Sigma}\ln\left(\prod\limits_{i=1}^n p(x_i\mid\boldsymbol\pi,\boldsymbol\mu,\boldsymbol\Sigma)\right)\\&=\operatorname*{argmax}_{\boldsymbol\pi,\boldsymbol\mu,\boldsymbol\Sigma}\sum_{i=1}^n\ln p(x_i\mid\boldsymbol\pi,\boldsymbol\mu,\boldsymbol\Sigma)\end{aligned}
likelihood-mixture | Insert the mixture inside each logarithm | For each row, first sum the possible Gaussian sources. Then take the log of that row’s mixture density. | The sum over $k$ combines different possible labels of a single row. The sum over $i$ combines different observed rows. The weight constraints keep the $\pi_k$ valid probabilities. | \begin{gathered}\operatorname*{argmax}_{\boldsymbol\pi,\boldsymbol\mu,\boldsymbol\Sigma}\sum_{i=1}^n\ln\left(\sum_{k=1}^K\pi_k\mathcal N(x_i;\mu_k,\Sigma_k)\right)\\\text{subject to }\quad\pi_k\ge 0\ (k=1,\ldots,K),\qquad\sum_{k=1}^K\pi_k=1\end{gathered}
likelihood-lagrange | Handle the weight-sum constraint | The Lagrange multiplier $\lambda$ accounts for the requirement that the weights sum to 1. | Setting the derivatives to zero gives equations that a regular interior stationary fit must satisfy. The updates use the current posterior weights $p_{ik}$, so the new mixture weights are automatically nonnegative. | \mathcal L=\sum_{i=1}^n\ln\left(\sum_{k=1}^K\pi_k\mathcal N(x_i;\mu_k,\Sigma_k)\right)-\lambda\left(\sum_{k=1}^K\pi_k-1\right)
responsibilities | Read $p_{ik}$ as a fractional assignment | Calculate the posterior for every row $i$ and every component $k$. A row can contribute partly to multiple components. | For a point with posteriors 0.8 and 0.2, its contributions to the components are 0.8 and 0.2 of an observation. Its row sums to 1. | p_{ik}=p_{Y\mid X}(k\mid x_i),\qquad \sum_{k=1}^K p_{ik}=1
mstep | Update 1 · Recompute each mean | Average the observations using their probabilities of belonging to this component. Divide by the total membership received. | $p_{ik}x_i$ is the weighted contribution of row $i$ to the mean of component $k$. Replacing the probabilities with 0 or 1 recovers the occupied-cluster mean from K-means. | \hat{\mu}_k=\frac{\sum_{i=1}^n p_{ik}x_i}{\sum_{i=1}^n p_{ik}}
mstep-cov | Update 2 · Recompute each covariance | Measure how the features spread around the updated mean, weighting each row by the same posterior as before. | The vector $x_i-\hat\mu_k$ has $m$ entries. Its outer product gives an $m\times m$ matrix of pairwise feature deviations. The weighted mean of these matrices gives the update. | \hat{\Sigma}_k=\frac{\sum_{i=1}^n p_{ik}(x_i-\hat\mu_k)(x_i-\hat\mu_k)^\top}{\sum_{i=1}^n p_{ik}}
mstep-weight | Update 3 · Recompute the mixture weights | Add up how much membership component $k$ receives, then divide by the number of observations. | Each row supplies exactly one unit of membership across all components. Therefore all updated weights must sum to 1. | \begin{gathered}\hat{\pi}_k=\frac{\sum_{i=1}^n p_{ik}}{n}\\\sum_{k=1}^K\hat{\pi}_k=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{k=1}^K\sum_{i=1}^n p_{ik}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n\sum_{k=1}^K p_{ik}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n1=1\end{gathered}
weighted-example | Follow one component’s three updates | Three one-dimensional observations sit at 0, 2 and 4. Change the membership of the rightmost observation and inspect the new mean, variance and mixture weight. | The effective count is the sum of the three memberships. In one feature the covariance matrix has one entry, the weighted variance. | %weighted
circular | Which do we need first, parameters or posteriors? | The posterior needs the current means, covariances and weights. Their new estimates need the posterior. Begin with initial parameters and alternate. | Calculate all memberships from the current mixture. Hold those memberships fixed while updating the parameters. Repeat until the fit stabilizes.
gmm-lab | Watch a Gaussian mixture fit the same cloud | Step through the fit, then inspect different observations. Compare their membership probabilities to the ellipses you see. | Ellipses summarize component covariance. A point has one plotted color for readability, while its posterior can spread over several components. | @gmm
em-limits | Convergence depends on the starting mixture | A fit can settle at a local solution. Centroids and assignments from K-means can give the Gaussian mixture a useful starting point. | Mixtures often need more iterations than K-means. In the full covariance model, a component can collapse onto one row and drive the likelihood arbitrarily high. The lab uses a small covariance regularizer.
@7.4 · Expectation-Maximization
em | Which information is observed and which is latent? | $\boldsymbol X$ contains the observed rows. $\boldsymbol Z$ contains their latent, unobserved information. In clustering, $z_i$ is the hidden group of observation $i$. | EM is useful when the complete-data likelihood $p(\boldsymbol X,\boldsymbol Z\mid\theta)$ is simpler than the observed-data likelihood $p(\boldsymbol X\mid\theta)$. We cannot plug in the missing $\boldsymbol Z$, so EM averages over its possible values. | \boldsymbol X=(x_1,\ldots,x_n),\qquad\boldsymbol Z=(z_1,\ldots,z_n),\qquad p(\boldsymbol X,\boldsymbol Z\mid\theta)
em-e | E-step · Estimate the missing memberships | Evaluate the distribution over latent values using the observed data and the old parameters. | In a Gaussian mixture this gives $p_{ik}$ for each row and component. $\theta^{(\mathrm{old})}$ holds all current model parameters. | p(\boldsymbol Z\mid\boldsymbol X,\theta^{(\mathrm{old})})
em-m | M-step · Choose the next parameters | With the current distribution of the hidden values held fixed, maximize the average complete-data log likelihood. | Read the expectation as a weighted average over possible assignments. The weights come from the E-step and stay fixed while the candidate $\theta$ varies. Set the old parameters to the new ones before the next E-step. | \theta^{(\mathrm{new})}=\operatorname*{argmax}_{\theta}\mathbb E_{\boldsymbol Z\mid\boldsymbol X,\theta^{(\mathrm{old})}}\,\left[\ln p(\boldsymbol X,\boldsymbol Z\mid\theta)\right]
em-kmeans | The same rhythm appears in K-means | Both methods alternate between deciding membership and updating cluster parameters. The mixture uses fractional memberships and K-means uses one-hot memberships. | In the small, shared spherical covariance limit with equal priors, the nearest Gaussian mean dominates away from ties. This relationship explains the hard-assignment analogue of the two-step procedure. | %alternation
"""),
    ("Hierarchical clustering", "Start with individual observations, build a dendrogram and decide where to cut it.",
     ["7.5 Hierarchical clustering", "7.5.1 Linkage method"],
     r"""
@7.5 · Hierarchical clustering
hierarchical | Can we build groups before choosing $K$? | K-means and Gaussian mixtures start with a group count. A hierarchy records how small groups combine into larger ones. | The agglomerative procedure learns a single sequence of nested partitions. Choose a partition after inspecting the tree or using a selection criterion.
near-pairs | Start with the nearest pair of observations | At the beginning every observation forms its own cluster. With one point in each cluster, any point-distance rule can compare the two. | Nearby observations are plausible first candidates for belonging together. Here the point-distance rule is Euclidean. | d(\{x_i\},\{x_{i^{\prime}}\})=\|x_i-x_{i^{\prime}}\|
singleton | Find the first merge yourself | Four singleton clusters start at 0, 2, 5 and 9. Inspect their pairwise distances and find the smallest one. | The shortest distance is 2 between the points at 0 and 2. Initially each pair is compared directly, so the choice among the listed linkage rules does not affect this first merge. | %first-merge
algorithm | The four actions that build the tree | 1. Put each observation in its own cluster. 2. Measure the distance between every pair of current clusters. 3. Join the two closest clusters. 4. Repeat steps 2 and 3 until only one cluster remains. | Begin with $n$ clusters, one for each of the $n$ observations. Each merge replaces two clusters with one, so there are $n-1$ merges in total.
merge-count | Watch four clusters become one | Step through a small example tree and count the groups remaining after each merge. | In a greedy agglomerative method, a group created at an earlier step stays together. The method can merge that group into a larger one. | %merge-count
dendrogram-source | Read the data and dendrogram together | Locate an observation on the left, then follow the leaf with the same label on the right. Which leaves join first? | This example has six leaves and five merges. The horizontal placement of leaves arranges the drawing. The meaningful distance is the height of the branch that joins them. | @source:hc_dendogram
leaves | What do the leaves and branches represent? | A leaf is one observation. A branch collects the observations reachable beneath it. The root contains the whole dataset. | Every node describes a group already built by the algorithm. Reading from the leaves to the root moves from finer to coarser partitions.
height | What does a merge height measure? | It is the distance between the two groups at the moment they were joined, under the chosen linkage rule. | Finding the first shared branch of two leaves tells you when they first became members of the same group. That height can differ from the original point-to-point distance.
cut | Cut at one height to obtain a partition | Draw a horizontal line across the tree. Every subtree separated by the line gives one group. | A low cut usually keeps many small groups. A higher cut typically allows more merges and yields fewer groups. With tied heights, a small change in threshold can include several merges together.
cut-example | Choose the cut and read the resulting groups | This example joins A and B at height 2, joins C and D at height 4, and joins the two pairs at height 7. Move the cut. | Our rule includes merges at or below the selected height. Just below 7 the groups are $\{A,B\}$ and $\{C,D\}$. At 7 the two pairs join. | %cut
select-cut | Where should we cut? | Look for a height before a large jump in merge distance. You can also compare the resulting candidate partitions with a criterion such as silhouette. | A large gap gives room to move the cut without changing the partition. Its interpretation still depends on the distance, linkage and goal. For silhouette, remove candidates with only one cluster or all singleton clusters.
@7.5.1 · Linkage method
linkage | Once a cluster has several points, what is its distance to another? | Suppose each cluster contains two points. The four cross-cluster distances are usually different. Linkage specifies how to turn them into one number. | $d(x_i,x_{i^{\prime}})$ is an observation distance. $d(C_k,C_{k^{\prime}})$ is a cluster distance defined by the linkage. The notation uses the same $d$ for these two related levels of comparison.
cross-pairs | List the distances between the groups | For $C_k=\{0,2\}$ and $C_{k^{\prime}}=\{5,9\}$, compare each member on the left with each member on the right. | Use absolute difference for these one-dimensional points. There are four cross-group pairs. Distances within either cluster are excluded. | \begin{matrix}d(0,5)=5 & d(0,9)=9\\d(2,5)=3 & d(2,9)=7\end{matrix}
complete-link | Complete linkage looks at the farthest pair | The distance between the groups is the largest distance across the two groups. In our running example that is 9. | A large value can come from a single widely separated pair. Complete linkage tends to favor more compact groups compared with single linkage. | d(C_k,C_{k^{\prime}})=\max\{d(x_i,x_{i^{\prime}})\mid x_i\in C_k,\ x_{i^{\prime}}\in C_{k^{\prime}}\}
single-link | Single linkage looks at the nearest pair | The distance between the groups is the smallest distance across the two groups. In our running example that is 3. | A single nearby pair is enough to make two groups close under this rule. In some datasets a chain of near neighbors can connect groups across a long region. | d(C_k,C_{k^{\prime}})=\min\{d(x_i,x_{i^{\prime}})\mid x_i\in C_k,\ x_{i^{\prime}}\in C_{k^{\prime}}\}
average-link | Average linkage uses all cross-group distances | Average the four distances. For the running example the result is $(5+9+3+7)/4=6$. | The denominator is the number of cross-group pairs. Each member of one cluster is paired once with each member of the other. | d(C_k,C_{k^{\prime}})=\frac{1}{|C_k||C_{k^{\prime}}|}\sum_{x_i\in C_k}\sum_{x_{i^{\prime}}\in C_{k^{\prime}}}d(x_i,x_{i^{\prime}})
centroid-link | Centroid linkage compares the two means | Replace each group by its mean. The centroids are 1 and 7 in our running example, so their distance is 6. | $\mu_k$ and $\mu_{k^{\prime}}$ are group mean vectors. The previous result also happened to be 6. Average and centroid linkage can differ when groups overlap or sit in different directions. | d(C_k,C_{k^{\prime}})=\|\mu_k-\mu_{k^{\prime}}\|
linkage-example | Reuse the same points with four distance rules | Pick a linkage and shift the second group. Compare which pairwise distances or centroids the rule uses. | Compare the average and centroid results after moving the second group across the first. Distances between averages and averages of distances are generally different. | %linkage
next-merge | The shortest cluster pair can change with the rule | After joining 0 with 2, compare the new cluster with 5 and compare the two remaining singletons at 5 and 9. | Single linkage gives a distance of 3 from $\{0,2\}$ to 5, so it merges those next. Complete linkage gives 5, so the singleton pair at distance 4 merges first. | %next-merge
hierarchy-lab | Build different trees from the same observations | Change linkage and adjust the merge count. Track whether the same points are grouped at the same stage. | The selected rule can change the merge order and heights. A dashed cut is shown where the current merge level can be represented by a horizontal cut. Centroid linkage can occasionally produce a lower height after a higher one. | @hierarchy
tree-compare | Compare the horizontal cut and the last merge | At a fixed height, different trees may give different partitions. To compare memberships at the same group count, inspect the merge count too. | For a usual monotone dendrogram, cutting higher never creates more groups. A centroid inversion is a case where merge height needs special care, so the recorded order remains the direct description of the sequence.
hierarchy-limits | What can the hierarchy tell us about our data? | A tree is a compact record of plausible groupings at different resolutions. Its structure reflects the features, point distance and linkage supplied. | Inspect disagreements across choices. Points repeatedly grouped together can be a useful signal. A group shown by one linkage alone can prompt a closer look.
group-recap | Connect the three ways of finding groups | K-means reports centroids and hard assignments. Gaussian mixtures add probabilities. Hierarchical clustering reports a tree from which we can select groups. | Each method answers a different question about the same observations. The next pack asks what the features themselves can tell us before or alongside any grouping.
"""),
    ("Feature analysis: principal components", "Find the directions in which observations vary most and use them for compression and visualization.",
     ["7.6 Feature analysis: principal components", "7.6.1 Applications"],
     r"""
@7.6 · Feature analysis: principal components
pca-intro | From analyzing groups to analyzing the feature space | Clustering organizes observations into groups. PCA looks for directions that describe how the observations vary. | A direction is a weighted combination of the original features. New axes can summarize a point cloud even when it has no supplied labels or obvious clusters.
aligned-lab | Start with a cloud lined up with a feature axis | Which coordinate has the larger spread? Inspect the cloud along $X_1$ and $X_2$. | Here $X_1$ captures more of the observed variation. It might contain more useful signal in this example, though high variance alone cannot establish what is useful for a task. | @source:gaussian_points
rotated-lab | Rotate the cloud and look for the same long direction | The long direction now lies between the two feature axes. Which slanted line follows it? | Looking at the variances of the two original features is no longer sufficient to identify the direction that best describes this cloud. | @source:gaussian_points_rot30
basis | Build an orthogonal basis ordered by variance | The first principal component follows the largest spread. Further components describe remaining spread in perpendicular directions. | $u_1,\ldots,u_m$ are mutually orthogonal unit directions. The component score of an observation is its coordinate on one of these directions. The name “component” can refer to the direction or its score, depending on context. | u_1,\ldots,u_m\in\mathbb R^m,\qquad u_j^\top u_{\ell}=\begin{cases}1&j=\ell\\0&j\ne\ell\end{cases}
projection | Project one observation onto a candidate direction | To measure variation along $u_1$, give every observation a single coordinate on that direction. | For unit $u_1$, the dot product is the signed length along the axis. The sign is determined by the direction of the arrow. In practice we subtract the mean to work in coordinates around the cloud’s center. | u_1^\top x_i
projection-example | Tilt the direction and watch one coordinate change | Rotate the candidate direction for the point $(3,1)$. Compare the signed score with the position of its perpendicular projection. | At 0° the coordinate is 3. At 90° it is 1. Some orientations place the point behind the origin along the axis and give a negative coordinate. | %projection
center | Projecting the mean gives the mean of the projections | The direction is fixed while the point changes, so the direction vector can move outside the sum. | The sample mean divides by $n$. Centering subtracts $\bar x$ from each row and makes the new mean zero in every direction. The $n-1$ divisor comes later, for the sample variance. | \bar x_{u_1}=\frac1n\sum_{i=1}^n u_1^\top x_i=u_1^\top\left(\frac1n\sum_{i=1}^n x_i\right)=u_1^\top\bar x
variance | What variance do the projected points have? | Measure the spread of the projected scores around their mean. The answer depends on the direction of projection. | $S$ is the sample covariance matrix of the original features. The scalar $s_{u_1}$ is the sample variance of their projection on $u_1$. | s_{u_1}=u_1^\top S u_1,\qquad S=\frac1{n-1}\sum_{i=1}^n(x_i-\bar x)(x_i-\bar x)^\top
variance-derivation | Derive the variance along a direction | Start with the scalar sample variance. Substitute the projection and mean, then factor the common direction out of the sum. | The square can be written as a product of a scalar with its transpose. Each centered outer product contributes one matrix to the sample covariance. | %variance-proof
unit-length | Why must the direction have length 1? | Stretching the arrow multiplies every score by the same factor. The measured variance grows even though the direction has stayed the same. | Restricting the length makes candidate directions comparable. Choose the one with the largest projected variance on the unit sphere. | \begin{gathered}\hat u_1=\operatorname*{argmax}_{u_1\in\mathbb R^m}u_1^\top S u_1\\\text{subject to }\|u_1\|=1\end{gathered}
unit-example | Stretch the arrow without tilting it | Change the vector length and inspect projected variance. Then change direction with length held fixed. | Multiplying $u_1$ by $c$ multiplies the variance by $c^2$. A longer arrow is a change of scale. A different angle is a change in what variation the projections capture. | %unit
pca-lagrange | Turn the unit-length requirement into an equation | Since a unit vector has $u_1^\top u_1=1$, the Lagrange multiplier $\lambda$ introduces that constraint into the stationary-point calculation. | For any feasible unit vector the added term is zero. It lets us derive the necessary condition for a best direction while keeping the length restriction. | \mathcal L(u_1,\lambda)=u_1^\top S u_1+\lambda(u_1^\top u_1-1)
eigen | A stationary direction is an eigenvector of $S$ | At a stationary solution the gradient in $u_1$ vanishes. Rearranging says that multiplying $u_1$ by $S$ simply scales it. | The eigenvalue is written $\lambda^{\prime}=-\lambda$ here to distinguish it from the multiplier in the previous line. The symmetric covariance matrix yields the derivative $2Su_1$. | \begin{aligned}2S u_1+2\lambda u_1&=0\\S u_1&=-\lambda u_1\\&=\lambda^{\prime}u_1\end{aligned}
eigen-variance | The eigenvalue tells us how much variance is captured | Multiply the eigenvector equation by $u_1^\top$ on the left. The unit length reduces the result to $\lambda^{\prime}$. | Each orthonormal eigenvector is a candidate direction. The eigenvalue is the variance of its scores, so the largest eigenvalue identifies the first component. | u_1^\top S u_1=\lambda^{\prime}u_1^\top u_1=\lambda^{\prime}
remaining-components | Order the rest of the components by their eigenvalues | A real symmetric covariance matrix has an orthonormal eigenbasis. Pick the remaining directions in descending variance order. | The eigenvalues are real and nonnegative. Reversing any component’s sign gives the same axis and variance. When eigenvalues tie, several orthonormal bases can describe the same eigenspace. | S u_j=\lambda_j u_j,\qquad\lambda_1\ge\lambda_2\ge\cdots\ge\lambda_m\ge0
pca-lab | Rotate the axis and see which direction wins | Rotate the projection and compare the explained variance and reconstruction error. Press Best PC to check your choice. | The highest spread along the line gives the first principal direction. For centered data this is also the one-dimensional axis with the smallest summed squared perpendicular reconstruction error. | @pca
scores | Once we have the directions, compute new coordinates | Subtract the original mean and take a dot product with each retained direction. These scores are the observation’s coordinates in the new basis. | $U_d$ has $d$ retained unit eigenvectors as columns. $z_i$ has $d$ scores, one for each retained component. | U_d=[u_1\ \cdots\ u_d],\qquad z_i=U_d^\top(x_i-\bar x)
variance-share | How much variance do the retained directions keep? | Add the first $d$ eigenvalues and divide by the sum of all eigenvalues. | The denominator is the total sample variance across all original features. This fraction presumes some nonzero variance. It measures retained spread, so a small-scale feature can still matter for a particular prediction task. | \frac{\sum_{j=1}^d\lambda_j}{\sum_{j=1}^m\lambda_j}
@7.6.1 · Applications
applications | Compression keeps the directions that carry most spread | Store fewer component scores per observation. A compact representation can still preserve much of the visible pattern, for example in some image datasets. | For an image, the observations might be images and the original features might be pixel values. A few directions can preserve much of the visible pattern. Keep the directions and original mean as well if you want to reconstruct the inputs.
reconstruct | Turn stored scores back into an approximate observation | Start from the data mean, then move along each retained direction by its score. | With all directions the basis reconstructs the centered data exactly. Dropping directions discards their coordinates, so reconstruction returns the projection onto the retained subspace, translated back to the original mean. | \hat x_i=\bar x+U_d z_i=\bar x+\sum_{j=1}^d z_{ij}u_j
compression-example | Try keeping one, two or three components | Select how many directions to store. Compare retained variance and the reconstruction of the same observation. | In this example the eigenvectors are coordinate axes. The eigenvalues are 9, 3 and 1. Each omitted score contributes an error along its omitted direction. | %compression
visualization | Use two or three scores to inspect high-dimensional data | A scatterplot of component scores gives a view of the strongest linear variation. | A group visible in this view can be investigated in the data. Overlapping groups may separate in other directions. Nonlinear visualization methods such as t-SNE can reveal different structure, with different interpretation rules.
preprocessing-bridge | What if the feature scales choose the direction for us? | A feature with much larger numerical spread can dominate PCA or a distance calculation. Preprocessing makes that choice explicit. | The next pack examines scaling and whitening. Neither operation is automatically best. The representation should match which differences matter in the task.
"""),
    ("PCA for Preprocessing", "Compare normalization, standardization and PCA whitening, and see how each changes the geometry of the data.",
     ["7.6.1.1 PCA for Preprocessing", "Normalization", "Standardization", "PCA Whitening"],
     r"""
@7.6.1.1 · PCA for Preprocessing
scale-source | One feature has a much larger numerical range | Compare the spread along $X_1$ and $X_2$. Which coordinate would contribute the most to a Euclidean distance on these axes? | In the example, the first feature has a wider numerical range. The colored groups are shown to help inspect the effect of changing representation. | @source:two_gaussian_blobs
distance-scale | How can unit choice change a distance? | Two observations differ by 100 on feature 1 and by 2 on feature 2. Their squared Euclidean distance is dominated by the first term. | If the first feature is measured in a different unit, its numerical difference can change without changing the underlying observation. This can change the neighbors selected by methods such as KNN. | \|x_i-x_{i^{\prime}}\|^2=100^2+2^2=10\,004
neighbor-scale | Can a new unit change the nearest neighbor? | Change the scale applied to feature 1 and look at which observation is closest to the query. | Scaling a feature multiplies its contribution to squared distance by the square of the scale factor. The same raw rows can yield different nearest neighbors. | %neighbor-scale
scaling | Choose which geometry the model will see | The three transformations address different problems. Normalization resets ranges. Standardization balances spread. PCA whitening also removes linear covariance. | Decide which effects are desirable for the task. A large range may be arbitrary because of units, or it may be important because the feature is measured on a meaningful common scale.
@7.6.1.1 · Normalization
normalization | Map the fitted feature range to $[0,1]$ | For each feature, subtract the observed minimum and divide by the fitted range. The smallest fitted value becomes 0 and the largest becomes 1. | The vector operations are elementwise. $x_{\min}$ and $x_{\max}$ hold a minimum and a maximum for each feature. This scaling is common for pixels in grayscale and RGB images, where bounds may be known in advance. | x_i\mapsto\frac{x_i-x_{\min}}{x_{\max}-x_{\min}}
normalization-example | See where a value lands in its fitted range | Move the value between or beyond the fitted endpoints 10 and 30. See the calculation and track its new position. | The original midpoint 20 maps to 0.5. A new value outside the fitted range can map outside $[0,1]$. The formula applies when the fitted range is nonzero. | %minmax
normalization-range | An extreme value can compress everything in between | Keep the ordinary observations fixed and move the maximum. Watch what happens to observations near the middle of the old range. | Dividing by a larger range makes the same original separation smaller. A constant feature has range zero, so it needs an explicit rule such as removal or mapping to zero. | %outlier
@7.6.1.1 · Standardization
standardization | Center each feature and scale its spread to one | Subtract the feature’s mean and divide by its standard deviation. A standardized value tells you how many standard deviations above or below the mean it lies. | $\boldsymbol S_{\mathrm{diag}}$ has the feature variances down its diagonal. Its inverse square root puts $1/\sigma_j$ in each entry on that diagonal. The output has unit variance under the same convention used for fitting. | x_i\mapsto\boldsymbol S_{\mathrm{diag}}^{-1/2}(x_i-\bar x)
standardization-calc | Compute the standardized value for one feature | A feature has mean 50 and standard deviation 10. Move the observation and read how far it is from the mean in standard-deviation units. | At 70 the standardized value is 2. At 40 it is $-1$. Each feature has its own mean and deviation. | %standardize
standardization-shape | What changes about the distribution’s shape? | Centering and rescaling move and stretch the same distribution. A Gaussian stays Gaussian and becomes standard Gaussian when its population mean and deviation are used. | A skewed or multimodal feature keeps its overall shape under this linear rescaling. Its mean and variance can be standardized even though the distribution is still skewed or multimodal. | %shape
standardized-cov | Why can standardization leave a tilted cloud? | Standardization rescales the original axes without rotating them. Two standardized features can still move together. | $Z_j$ and $Z_{j^{\prime}}$ are the standardized features. If both original variances are positive, their standardized covariance equals the original correlation. Whitening addresses that remaining linear association. | \operatorname{Cov}(Z_j,Z_{j^{\prime}})=\frac{\operatorname{Cov}(X_j,X_{j^{\prime}})}{\sigma_j\sigma_{j^{\prime}}}
@7.6.1.1 · PCA Whitening
whitening | Rotate first, then put the component spreads on equal footing | Project the centered observation onto the PCA directions. Divide each resulting score by the square root of its eigenvalue. | $\boldsymbol U$ contains the unit eigenvectors as columns. $\boldsymbol L$ is diagonal with the eigenvalues in the same order. Keep directions with positive eigenvalues for this inverse. | x_i\mapsto\boldsymbol L^{-1/2}\boldsymbol U^\top(x_i-\bar x)
whitening-rotate | Step 1 rotates to axes where covariance is zero | $\boldsymbol U^\top$ expresses the centered observations on the principal axes. The resulting scores can still have different variances. | The $j$-th diagonal entry of $\boldsymbol L$ is the variance $\lambda_j$ of principal component $j$. All off-diagonal entries are zero. | \boldsymbol U^\top S\boldsymbol U=\boldsymbol L
whitening-scale | Step 2 rescales each component to unit variance | Divide the $j$-th component’s scores by its standard deviation $\sqrt{\lambda_j}$. The zero off-diagonal entries stay zero. | On the retained positive-variance components, the adjustment turns every diagonal entry into 1. Therefore the covariance becomes the identity $I$. | \boldsymbol L^{-1/2}\boldsymbol L\boldsymbol L^{-1/2}=I
whitening-calc | Whiten one pair of PCA scores | The component variances are 9 and 1. Change the scores and follow how far each lies from the center on the whitened scale. | Dividing the first score by 3 and the second by 1 gives the whitened coordinates. The larger original component variance corresponds to stronger shrinkage. | %whiten
whitening-dependence | What does zero covariance tell us? | The whitened components have no pairwise linear covariance. Nonlinear dependence can remain. | For example, a symmetric centered variable $V$ can have zero covariance with $V^2$ even though $V^2$ is determined by $V$. If correlation is useful signal for the task, removing it can be undesirable.
whitening-small | What happens when a component barely varies? | A very small eigenvalue produces a large multiplier. Whitening may amplify measurement noise along directions with little variation. | An exact zero cannot be inverted. Keep a positive-variance subspace, or regularize deliberately if the task calls for it. The full identity-covariance statement applies to the unregularized retained subspace with positive eigenvalues. | %small-eigenvalue
preprocessing-lab | Compare the same observations in all four views | Switch between Original, Min–max, Standardized and Whitened. Compare the ranges and the covariance shown beside each cloud. | All views use the same observations. The standardized and whitened panels were fitted with a population-variance convention. The displayed sample covariance uses $n-1$ in the denominator, so its unit variances appear slightly above 1. | @panel:two_gaussian_blobs_panel
compare-pipeline | Identify what changed from one panel to the next | Min–max adjusts ranges. Standardization adjusts the mean and spread feature by feature. Whitening rotates and adjusts the spread of components. | The labels on the whitened axes are component scores. Labels on the two rescaled panels still refer to the original features in rescaled coordinates.
fit-transform | Fit the transformation once and reuse it | Learn the means, ranges, variances or PCA basis on the training rows. Apply those same values to validation, test and future rows. | A new point should be transformed with the representation that the model saw in training. Refitting on evaluation data leaks information and changes the meaning of its coordinates.
workflow | Use the representation that suits the goal | Change units when ranges overwhelm distance. Use PCA when a compact linear view helps. Use whitening when the task benefits from unit variance and zero linear covariance. | Re-check the geometry after transforming the data. Follow the priorities of the task and use validation if comparing downstream predictive performance.
practice | Read the evidence before choosing a transformation | Try three situations where you care about different properties of the data. Compare the suggested transformations and why they fit. | A different application goal can justify a different answer. Say what should stay meaningful after the data are transformed. | %choose-transform
summary | Different transformations answer different questions | Ask whether the original units are meaningful, whether covariance should be retained, and which comparisons the transformed model will make. | Across all five packs, clustering methods organize rows while PCA and preprocessing change the representation of rows. Both choices influence what structure is visible.
"""),
]
